        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 7 Getting to Know the Linux Filesystem\nP\u00e1ginas: 137-150\n\u2022 Pieces of the Puzzle  (137)\n\u2022 Touring the Linux Filesystem  (138)\n  \u2022 The root of the tree  (138)\n  \u2022 The importance of being /etc  (140)\n  \u2022 Where removable media lives  (140)\n  \u2022 Where the computer is the /usr  (141)\n\u2022 Managing Your Filesystem without a Net (or Mouse)  (141)\n  \u2022 Viewing information about files on the command line  (142)\n  \u2022 Understanding file listing information  (143)\n  \u2022 Comprehending file types  (145)\n  \u2022 Navigating the filesystem in Linux  (146)\n\u2022 A Permissions Primer  (147)\n  \u2022 Checking out the triplets  (147)\n  \u2022 Beware of owners  (149)\n  \u2022 Hanging out in groups  (149)\nChapter 7\nGetting to Know the\nLinux Filesystem\nO\nne of the most frustrating things about mastering a new operating system\ncan be figuring out where it keeps files. Instead of keeping all important\nsystem files in a single directory, such as the C:\\Windows directory in\nMicrosoft Windows, Linux follows the lead of its UNIX cousins and spreads out\nthings a bit more. Although the Linux and Windows setups involve different\nmethods, they are both logical, although it may not feel that way until you under-\nstand where to look.\nAfter taking you on a tour of where to find things, this chapter shows you how to\nwork in your filesystem on the command line. Reading this section isn\u2019t manda-\ntory if typing commands gives you heart palpitations, but it can be handy if you\nneed to fix something later, and some people just like to know how to do these\nthings.",
